A||||6||Shiner||Drip Rail Removal||||1||
Z||000000||Shiner||12-05-2000||01:24 AM||||Just what it says in the topic  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Can this be done?  How?  I am getting ready to paint, and I am realising it will be hard to sand with those suckers there.  Anybody know?<P>------------------<BR>-Shiner-<BR>'84 Sport Coupe<BR>'87 GT<BR>'86 SE 2M6 (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000001||Standard||12-05-2000||02:38 AM||||Just pull them straight out from the body.  Start at one end and work you way towards the other end.  They're held on by little tabs that are just pushed under the body panels.  They bend easy, too, so take it easy.||||reg||1
Z||000002||2M4 Dale||12-05-2000||03:56 AM||||I have removed them by gently holding a small block of soft wood (pine) thats about 1"X 3" against the drip rail and taping lightly with a small hammer......If you start at on end and be gentle you can remove them without damage.||||reg||6
Z||000003||1FST2M6||12-05-2000||07:09 AM||||i pulled mine out before the car was painted and put my new 1/4 windows in before i put the rail back in.. I like the looks of it with out the rails but like others have said... "you can't drive in the rain with the window down" i'm not a smoker so that doens't pertain to me... I like it.   just grab the end and pulll straight down working you way to the other end. they are pretty flinsy and will fold pretty easily too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Nashco||05-20-2001||12:41 AM||||I'm considering ditching my drip rails for a couple reasons.  Number one is they are loose and rattle sometimes (I know I can fix this if I wanted to).  Number two is because they look kinda goofy.  Number three is they are hard to get the car clean with them on.  Number four, I don't drive with the window down while it's wet...I'm not stupid <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Do we have any opinions on this?  I haven't taken mine off yet, and was hoping to get some feedback on this "mod" before doing it.  Anybody have pics, that would be cool!<P>Thanks,<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||fropuff||05-20-2001||01:08 AM||||Well, I guess I wouldn't really call it a "mod", but it looks pretty good without the rails.  It doesn't really matter if you drive in the rain w/ the windows open, but it does when you drive in the rain and go to open the door.  You're lucky if only half the water on the roof goes onto the glass, the door panel, the seat, the carpet, your head, shirt, etc.  If you want to see what it's like w/o them, just take the little bastards off.  Unless you don't drive in the rain, it won't take long before you put em back on.  Do be careful when taking them off.  Believe it or not, there's a clip near the rear end of the rail, the pointy end by the sail panel.  I didn't think there was, and pulled the rail out.  The clip was still attached, so it bent the little end until it busted off.  That's why I need a new one.  One more thing: when you have em off, put them up high.  I had mine on the ground, and I kneeled on one.  I  had to hammer the dent out and now get to paint it.  Have fun!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000006||Taijiguy||05-20-2001||09:07 AM||||I'm with fropuff on this one. I pulled mine off so I could sand the roof. We've had a week solid of rain here, and I can't wait to put them back on. You can't imagine how much water they divert until you take them off. I put my window down a half inch to help defog the windshield, and the first sign of a right hand turn, the water gushed in. <BR>Like has been said: Take them off if you have any question. You'll be putting them back on. If you find them unappealing, paint them to match the car (that's what I'm doing) it will make them less obtrusive.<P>Oh, and the best way I've found to <I>(temporarily)</I> remove them is to place a narrow putty knife down into the channel and tap it with a hammer to break it loose, and work my way along till it's free. Haven't bent one yet that way...<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 05-20-2001).]||||reg||1
